Merely to pretend that I'm not as lazy as I really
am, I went and looked up the message in the HTTP 1.1
standard document, at

http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc2616/rfc2616-sec10.html#sec10.5.3

where it says:

:- 10.5.3 502 Bad Gateway

:- The server, while acting as a gateway or proxy,
:- received an invalid response from the upstream
:- server it accessed in attempting to fulfill the
:- request.

The error name is a bit misleading compared to the
descriptive message; it would be better named
"Gateway Response Error" instead. "Bad Gateway"
implies a persisting problem with a gateway, which
is not necessarily the case; it could as easily be a
one time noise burst problem.

If not, though, something really is broken. If the
upstream server groks HTTP at all, it should be
sending valid error messages, not invalid responses,
in the case where it has problems with the packets
it is receiving from the server nearer to the user.
If it doesn't, it shouldn't be providing an open
HTTP socket in the first place.

